@Riteo can you make an example? "Proper" empty objects like the one in Blender are very useful (as they have a position and orientation), but in obj empty objects don't really have anything?

Also, Godot does not really like empty meshes and prints various error messages if a mesh gets empty. So these empty meshes may cause serious issues down the road.

That said, if it helps some use case, I'm happy to only skip over whitespace before the first object. That is the main practical issue imho: every obj file that consists solely of named objects gets an additional, unnamed and empty mesh. I can't imagine that one being of use to anybody?

@rsjtdrjgfuzkfg

> but in obj empty objects don't really have anything?

Oh, I had no idea. If that's the case then I'm not sure that might be a valid usecase specifically with OBJ.

@Riteo I reacted with a thumbs up to your doubt because I was also wondering if using empty objects as a pivot-fix-hack is a valid usecase.

It was only a few days before your comment that I was working with an artist importing an FBX into godot and the objects had the wrong pivot, so your comment got me thinking if using empty objects is a potential workaround, but honestly I have not explored that workaround and I do not think supporting empty objects is the best way to fix pivots.

I would prefer we open a separate issue for incorrect object pivots and I can try to make a PR to fix that properly, not using this hacky empty objects way since this is a divergence from the normal artist workflow.
